# phpup-2026 - Suricata rules
# CVE-2026-109618 / CVE-2026-109619 · PHP longjmp/bailout state corruption · RCE
#
# 2026-10-21 rewrite. The prior revision of this file keyed on a
# single-request multipart-stall heap-spray mechanism (149-field spray,
# forged zend_fcall_info at fixed byte offsets, >40KB body threshold) that
# was derived from the leaked PoC artifact's own claims. That mechanism
# does not match the real fix (php/php-src commit 82f8f31b16a, "Avoid
# state corruption via bailout") and those rules have been deleted, not
# caveated - see ANALYSIS.md. The real mechanism is cross-request: a
# longjmp-interrupted request leaves worker-persistent globals dangling,
# consumed by a LATER, DIFFERENT request on the SAME worker process.
# That "same worker" condition is not visible from HTTP/network logs at
# all, so most of what's left here is the one piece that genuinely is
# network-visible: the OOM-amplification technique on the producer
# request (main/rfc1867.c's now-fixed unbounded per-part header count).
#
# One rule per physical line below - Suricata requires this (confirmed
# against 7.0.3). `bsize`, not `dsize`, for the reassembled
# http.request_body buffer (dsize is a raw per-packet match and cannot be
# combined with http.* sticky buffers). A literal `;` inside a PCRE
# character class (e.g. `[!-9;-~]`) also breaks Suricata's own rule-option
# tokenizer, even quoted inside the pcre value -- confirmed 2026-10-21
# against 7.0.3 with a 3-rule minimal reproduction; use \x3b instead.
#
# 2026-10-21 full re-verification: every rule below was actually run with
# `suricata -S ... -r <pcap> -k none` against all four real captures in
# examples/pcaps/ (not just reasoned about). `-k none` is required for the
# two loopback-captured pcaps (oom-amplification, fpm-worker-recycle-85)
# -- Suricata rejects their packets as checksum-invalid by default because
# loopback capture skips real checksum computation; the two anonymized
# captures (benign-upload-progress, stall-no-crash) don't have this
# artifact. Results: 9109627 fired exactly once on each of the two
# captures that should trigger it and zero times elsewhere; 9109633 fired
# exactly twice on each of the two crash+survive captures (once per
# payload-bearing request, zero times on the benign request in the same
# capture) and zero times on the two captures with no such payload.
# 9109635 never fired in any of the four captures -- none of them happen
# to exercise it, so at that point it remained unverified, not confirmed.
# 9109636 fired 325 and 288 times respectively in captures containing
# 2-3 requests total -- proven non-functional, retired below with the
# real counts.
#
# 2026-10-21, later the same day: built a real, legitimate 4-file
# upload (examples/pcaps/phpup2026-real-legit-multifile-benign.pcap --
# each file 3 bytes of real content, not empty, server responded 200 OK
# with all four files received intact) specifically to check two open
# questions. Result: 9109633 did NOT fire on it -- confirms the PCRE's
# negative lookahead `(?!\x0d\x0a)` correctly treats the blank line that
# MIME requires between every part's headers and its body as a hard
# stop, so it cannot cross from one legitimate part's Content-Disposition
# into a different part's, regardless of file count or content size; not
# an incidental result of this repo's earlier captures happening to have
# large binary content between parts. 9109635 DID fire on it (see
# examples/logs/phpup2026-eve-legit-multifile-fp.json) -- a real,
# reproducible false positive on ordinary legitimate traffic, not a gap
# in test coverage. Retired below with the real evidence, same as
# 9109634/9109636.
#
# SID range 9109627, 9109633 (9109628-9109632, 9109634, 9109635, 9109636 retired below)

# --- feature-presence / policy (low severity, high recall) ---

# Precondition for the session.upload_progress consumer path specifically:
# PHP_SESSION_UPLOAD_PROGRESS field name present alongside a PHPSESSID
# cookie in a multipart POST. This is also how the legitimate
# upload-progress-bar feature is used, so it is inventory/policy
# visibility (who still has session.upload_progress reachable from the
# internet), not an attack signal by itself. Unaffected by the mechanism
# correction -- this is just "is the feature reachable."
alert http $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HTTP_SERVERS any (msg:"PHPUP2026 session.upload_progress feature in use (multipart POST + PHPSESSID)"; flow:established,to_server; http.method; content:"POST"; http.header; content:"multipart/form-data"; nocase; http.cookie; content:"PHPSESSID="; nocase; http.request_body; content:"PHP_SESSION_UPLOAD_PROGRESS"; fast_pattern; classtype:policy-violation; sid:9109627; rev:1;)

# --- OOM-amplification producer signal (medium-high confidence) ---
#
# 2026-10-21 fix: this rule originally pattern-matched the literal string
# "Content-Disposition" duplicated. Verified against this repo's own
# real OOM-amplification capture (examples/pcaps/
# phpup2026-real-oom-amplification.pcap) that it DID NOT FIRE -- the real
# vulnerable code (main/rfc1867.c pre-fix) saves ANY header line
# regardless of name, so the actual reproduction used a short arbitrary
# name ("A:0" x1.4M) for maximum entries-per-byte, not literally
# "Content-Disposition" repeated. A rule keyed on one literal header name
# is under-inclusive against a competent attacker optimizing for
# amplification density. Replaced with a generic "same header name
# appears twice in one part" match via a PCRE backreference -- re-run
# against the real capture after this fix and confirmed it now fires
# (and does not fire on the paired benign capture). See
# examples/INDEX.md for the verification record. 2026-10-21, later the
# same day: also confirmed it does NOT false-positive on a real
# legitimate 4-file upload (examples/pcaps/
# phpup2026-real-legit-multifile-benign.pcap) -- the MIME blank-line
# between every part's headers and body stops the backreference from
# ever crossing from one legitimate part's Content-Disposition into
# another's, independent of file count or content size.
alert http $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HTTP_SERVERS any (msg:"PHPUP2026 duplicate header name in one multipart part (OOM-amplification attempt)"; flow:established,to_server; http.method; content:"POST"; http.header; content:"multipart/form-data"; nocase; http.request_body; pcre:"/\x0d\x0a([\x21-\x39\x3b-\x7e]+):[ \t]?[^\x0d\x0a]*(?:\x0d\x0a(?!\x0d\x0a)(?!\1:)[^\x0d\x0a]*){0,20}\x0d\x0a\1:/"; classtype:web-application-attack; sid:9109633; rev:3;)

# 9109634 (density proxy, "3+ occurrences") retired 2026-10-21: an
# attempted extension of the 9109633 pattern to require a third
# occurrence did not match its own should-match test cases in Python
# (re.search) before it was ever deployed -- caught in testing, not
# shipped broken. A correct N-occurrence generic rule needs more
# PCRE engineering than a simple backreference chain; not worth
# shipping a second, weaker rule when 9109633 alone is verified working.
# Revisit if real traffic shows 9109633 alone needs a coarser companion.

# --- retired (2026-10-21) ---
#
# 9109628 oversized multipart body (>40000 bytes) -- built on the dead
#   "needs many 288-byte reclaim blobs" spray-count theory. Body-size
#   alone is not a validated signal for the real mechanism.
# 9109629 dangerous-function string in upload_progress body -- assumed
#   a same-request forged-callback payload is planted directly in the
#   HTTP body. No basis for this in the real mechanism; retired.
# 9109630 forged zend_fcall_info / zval byte markers (IS_STRING_EX /
#   IS_OBJECT_EX) -- this entire byte layout came from the leaked PoC
#   artifact's own code, which VulnCheck reports does not have the
#   correct mechanism. Retired, not just caveated.
# 9109631/9109632 hostbit-correlation pair anchored on 9109630 -- dead
#   along with its anchor.
# 9109635 "multiple empty file fields in multipart body" -- retired
#   2026-10-21 after a real test with a genuinely legitimate 4-file
#   upload (examples/pcaps/phpup2026-real-legit-multifile-benign.pcap,
#   each file 3 bytes of real content, server responded 200 OK) fired
#   this rule anyway (examples/logs/phpup2026-eve-legit-multifile-fp.json).
#   The rule's own content match -- three "filename=" occurrences within
#   500 bytes -- never actually checked for emptiness, despite the
#   rule's name and the advisory text it was supposedly modeling; any
#   legitimate form with 3+ small file fields close together triggers
#   it. A correct version would need to confirm zero bytes of body
#   between each part's header blank-line and the next boundary, which
#   needs more PCRE engineering than this rule had -- not worth
#   reshipping without that fix. Proven false-positiving on ordinary
#   traffic, not just unverified; retired with the real evidence, same
#   standard as 9109634/9109636 above.
# 9109636 "outbound connection from HTTP server shortly after a PHP
#   worker fatal-error response" -- retired 2026-10-21 after live-testing
#   against this repo's own real-capture pcaps (full re-verification
#   pass, see examples/INDEX.md): it has no content match at all, just
#   $HTTP_SERVERS -> $EXTERNAL_NET + flow:to_server,established, so it
#   fired 325 times in phpup2026-real-oom-amplification.pcap and 288
#   times in phpup2026-real-fpm-worker-recycle-85.pcap -- both captures
#   contain only 2-3 HTTP requests total. That is not a correlatable
#   signal, it is "a TCP connection existed." A replacement needs a
#   validated primary signal to anchor on first; the better-grounded
#   anchor we do have now is host-based, not network-based: php-fpm
#   logging "exited with code 70 after N seconds from start" followed
#   by a new child PID handling the next request on the same pool. See
#   iocs.json's host_indicators section for exactly what that exit code
#   does and doesn't confirm (2026-10-21 correction: it's one specific
#   fpm_main.c check, not a distinct "unrecoverable" engine-level path).
